<PaperLayout
  title="DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning"
  eyebrow="Reasoning LLM · RL · 2025 / arXiv"
  authors="DeepSeek-AI"
  arxivUrl="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948"
  subtitle="A structured reading of the paper that separates the scientific R1-Zero claim from the product-oriented DeepSeek-R1 training report."
  sections={sections}
  annotations={annotations}
  initialAnnotation="reasoning"
  compactTitle={true}
>
        <section id="overview" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Overview</h2>

          <div class="guide-box">
            <strong>Reading guide</strong>
            <p>
              Read this paper as two documents interleaved together. One document is a technical argument about
              <button class="anno" data-anno="reasoning">reasoning capability</button>: can reinforcement learning
              make a base LLM discover better reasoning behavior without first imitating human-written reasoning traces?
              The other document is a model report about a public product model: how DeepSeek-R1 was trained, evaluated,
              distilled, released, and safety-checked.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The most important technical object is <button class="anno" data-anno="r1zero">DeepSeek-R1-Zero</button>.
            It is trained directly from <button class="anno" data-anno="deepseekV3Base">DeepSeek-V3-Base</button>
            using <button class="anno" data-anno="grpo">GRPO</button> and <button class="anno" data-anno="reward">rule-based rewards</button>. It is not polished,
            but it is the paper's cleanest evidence that RL can incentivize <button class="anno" data-anno="cot">long chain-of-thought</button>,
            self-checking, reflection, and strategy switching.
          </p>

          <p>
            The final model, <button class="anno" data-anno="r1">DeepSeek-R1</button>, answers a different question:
            after raw reasoning behavior appears, how do we turn it into a readable, useful assistant? That requires
            <button class="anno" data-anno="coldStart">cold-start data</button>, more RL,
            <button class="anno" data-anno="rejectionSampling">rejection sampling</button>, large-scale
            <button class="anno" data-anno="sft">SFT</button>, preference rewards, and safety mechanisms.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
            <span>Strong base model</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Verifiable problems</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Rule rewards</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>GRPO</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Long-CoT reasoning</span>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="why" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Why this paper exists</h2>

          <p>
            The background problem is that strong reasoning models before R1 were often associated with heavy
            supervision, carefully crafted reasoning traces, or closed training recipes. The standard post-training
            story was roughly: first use SFT to teach the model how good answers look, then use RL or preference
            optimization to align the model further.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeepSeek-R1 asks whether that order hides a limitation. Human-written chain-of-thought can make models
            readable and controllable, but it also tells the model what kind of reasoning to imitate. If human traces
            omit verification, backtracking, or strange but useful non-human search patterns, SFT may cap exploration.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">The paper's central bet</span>
            <p>
              For tasks where the final answer can be checked reliably, we may not need to teach the model every
              reasoning step. We can give it hard problems, a reliable verifier, and enough RL compute. Then successful
              reasoning behaviors may become more likely because they lead to correct final answers.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is why the word <em>incentivizing</em> in the title matters. The authors are not primarily saying,
            "we wrote better reasoning demonstrations." They are saying, "we designed a reward environment where useful
            reasoning behavior becomes advantageous."
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="map" class="reading-section">
          <h2>R1-Zero vs R1</h2>

          <p>
            A lot of confusion disappears once you separate R1-Zero from R1. R1-Zero is closer to a controlled research
            experiment. R1 is closer to a productized assistant model.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Model</th>
