        <section id="grpo" class="reading-section">
          <h2>GRPO mechanics</h2>

          <p>
            <button class="anno" data-anno="grpo">GRPO</button> is the algorithmic engine, but the paper is not a GRPO
            theory paper. The important question is how GRPO makes final-answer rewards usable for long chain-of-thought
            training.
          </p>

          <p>
            In <button class="anno" data-anno="ppo">PPO-style RL</button> for LLMs, one usually trains a <button class="anno" data-anno="policy">policy model</button> and a value model.
            The value model estimates how much reward to expect from a partial response. That is expensive for very large models and conceptually hard
            for long CoT: a partial solution may look wrong, then later be corrected; or it may look right, then collapse.
          </p>

          <p>
            GRPO removes the separate value model. Instead, for each question, it samples a group of candidate answers.
            Each answer gets a reward. The algorithm then asks: which sampled answer was better than the group's average?
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>GRPO component</th>
                  <th>What it does</th>
                  <th>Why it matters for R1-Zero</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {grpoRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.concept}</td>
                    <td>{row.explanation}</td>
                    <td>{row.why}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">The key mental model</span>
            <p>
              GRPO turns one prompt into a small competition among the model's own answers. The answers that beat the
              local group average get reinforced; the answers that fall below the local group average get discouraged.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="math" class="reading-section">
          <h2>GRPO math</h2>

          <p>
            The paper's GRPO objective can look intimidating, but it is built from a few simple pieces. For a question
            <code>q</code>, sample a group of outputs from the old policy:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              {'{'}o<sub>1</sub>, o<sub>2</sub>, ..., o<sub>G</sub>{'}'} ~ π<sub>θ_old</sub>(O | q)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            Score every output with the reward function:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              {'{'}r<sub>1</sub>, r<sub>2</sub>, ..., r<sub>G</sub>{'}'}
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            Then compute a standardized group-relative advantage:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              A<sub>i</sub> = (r<sub>i</sub> − mean({'{'}r<sub>1</sub>, ..., r<sub>G</sub>{'}'})) / std({'{'}r<sub>1</sub>, ..., r<sub>G</sub>{'}'})
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is the most important formula in the section. A positive <button class="anno" data-anno="advantage">advantage</button>
            means the output was better than its peers for the same prompt. A negative advantage means it was worse.
            This replaces PPO's learned value model with a group baseline.
          </p>

          <p>
            The update then compares the current policy probability with the old <button class="anno" data-anno="rollout">rollout</button> probability:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              ratio<sub>i</sub> = π<sub>θ</sub>(o<sub>i</sub> | q) / π<sub>θ_old</sub>(o<sub>i</sub> | q)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            If <code>A<sub>i</sub> &gt; 0</code>, increasing this ratio is good: the model should become more likely to produce
            this kind of answer. If <code>A<sub>i</sub> &lt; 0</code>, increasing the ratio is bad: the model should move away from
            this answer. Clipping prevents the ratio from changing too aggressively.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              min(ratio<sub>i</sub>A<sub>i</sub>, clip(ratio<sub>i</sub>, 1−ε, 1+ε)A<sub>i</sub>) − βD<sub>KL</sub>(π<sub>θ</sub> || π<sub>ref</sub>)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            The final term is the KL penalty. It discourages the current policy from drifting too far from the reference
            policy. Without this anchor, a reward-maximizing language model can become unstable, repetitive, or overly
            specialized to the reward format.
          </p>

          <div class="concept-grid">
            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Positive advantage</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula"><code>A<sub>i</sub> &gt; 0</code></div>
              <p>The response scored above the group average. GRPO pushes the model to make it more likely.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Negative advantage</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula"><code>A<sub>i</sub> &lt; 0</code></div>
              <p>The response scored below the group average. GRPO pushes the model to make it less likely.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>No value model</h3>
              <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula"><code>group mean ≈ baseline</code></div>
              <p>The group reward distribution substitutes for a learned critic, saving memory and simplifying long-CoT RL.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="rewards" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Reward design</h2>

          <p>
            The paper's real technical bottleneck is not the GRPO formula alone. It is the <button class="anno" data-anno="reward">reward</button>. RL only optimizes what
            the reward signal measures. If the reward is reliable, the model can learn useful reasoning. If the reward is flawed, the model can learn shortcuts.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Reward</th>
                  <th>Used in</th>
                  <th>How it works</th>
                  <th>Risk or limitation</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {rewardRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.reward}</td>
                    <td>{row.usedIn}</td>
                    <td>{row.how}</td>
                    <td>{row.risk}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            R1-Zero uses a deliberately simple <button class="anno" data-anno="ruleReward">rule-based reward</button>:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              Reward<sub>rule</sub> = Reward<sub>accuracy</sub> + Reward<sub>format</sub>
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is why verifiable tasks matter. In math, code, and multiple-choice reasoning, the final answer can often
            be checked automatically by a <button class="anno" data-anno="verifier">verifier</button>. The model is not
            told exactly how to reason; it is rewarded when its final answer is correct and its output is parseable.
          </p>

          <p>
            The appendix's <button class="anno" data-anno="rewardHacking">reward hacking</button> example is a warning:
            when a learned helpfulness reward model is optimized too long, the reward can keep increasing while real
            Codeforces performance decreases. This is one reason the paper treats rule-based rewards as the backbone of
            reasoning RL.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="data" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Data and verifiers</h2>

          <p>
            R1-Zero is often summarized as "pure RL," but the training environment is carefully chosen. The prompts are
            mostly from domains where correctness can be checked. That is what makes outcome-based RL practical.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Category</th>
