                    <td>{row.task}</td>
                    <td>{row.verifier}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            This table is the practical boundary of the method. Math and code are good RL domains because they produce
            crisp feedback. Writing and open-ended dialogue are harder because there is no single correct answer. In those
            domains, R1 uses SFT and reward models, but the paper is much more cautious about reward hacking.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="rl-loop" class="reading-section">
          <h2>From GRPO formula to training loop</h2>

          <p>
            The GRPO objective explains what is optimized, but the paper also gives an important engineering view of how
            reinforcement learning is actually executed at scale. The figure contains rollout workers,
            reward modules, training modules, and VRAM management. For understanding the paper, the essential loop is simpler:
            generate several attempts, score them, compare them within the group, and update the policy.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">The scalable RL loop</span>

