            <p>
              This is the operational meaning of GRPO in R1-Zero. The system does not need a human-written reasoning trace for
              each problem. It needs a strong base model, many sampled attempts, and reliable ways to score the final outcome.
              Once the rewards are available, GRPO turns the group comparison into a policy update.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="comparison-grid">
            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Rollout</strong>
              <p>
                The policy model receives reasoning prompts and samples multiple completions for the same question. In R1-Zero,
                this group of completions is the raw material for learning: some attempts will solve the problem, some will fail,
                and GRPO learns from the contrast between them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Scoring</strong>
              <p>
                The outputs are scored by a combination of rule-based checks and model-based components. For the core reasoning
                tasks, the most important signals are verifiable: final-answer matching for math, test execution for code, and
                format checking for the required response structure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Group advantage</strong>
              <p>
                Instead of asking a value model to estimate future reward, GRPO compares outputs within the same group. A
                response receives positive advantage if its reward is above the group average and negative advantage if it is
                below the group average.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Policy update</strong>
              <p>
                The training step increases the probability of high-advantage outputs and decreases the probability of
                low-advantage outputs, while a KL term keeps the model from drifting too far from a reference policy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="evolution" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Self-evolution evidence</h2>

          <p>
            The most interesting claim is behavioral: RL does not merely raise benchmark accuracy; it changes how the model
            solves problems. During R1-Zero training, the average response length increases, and the model begins to use
            more reflective language such as <em>wait</em>, <em>check</em>, <em>verify</em>, <em>wrong</em>, and <em>retry</em>.
          </p>

          <div class="concept-grid">
            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Longer thinking</h3>
              <p>
                The model learns to spend more tokens before final answers. Length itself is not directly the reward;
                longer reasoning survives because it helps produce correct answers on hard problems.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Reflection</h3>
              <p>
                The model increasingly generates phrases that indicate checking and correction. This suggests that RL
                reinforces behaviors that help the model recover from wrong intermediate paths.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="concept-card">
              <h3>Adaptive difficulty</h3>
              <p>
                Later analysis shows that harder problems tend to receive more thinking tokens, connecting long CoT to
                test-time compute rather than simple verbosity.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The famous "aha moment" should not be read as proof of human-like consciousness. It is better read as a
            generated behavior pattern that becomes useful under the reward environment. The model learns that pausing,
            reconsidering, and revising can lead to correct final answers.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="experiments" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Benchmarks and metrics</h2>

          <p>
            The experiment section can feel like a product scoreboard. Benchmarks such as <button class="anno" data-anno="aime">AIME</button>, <button class="anno" data-anno="livecodebench">LiveCodeBench</button>, and <button class="anno" data-anno="gpqa">GPQA</button> are useful
            only if we read them as evidence for different kinds of reasoning. For a technical reading, focus on what each benchmark is testing and why it matters for the R1-Zero-to-R1 story.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Benchmark</th>
                  <th>Type</th>
                  <th>What it measures</th>
                  <th>Why it matters</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {benchmarkRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.name}</td>
                    <td>{row.type}</td>
                    <td>{row.measures}</td>
                    <td>{row.why}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two metrics are especially important. <button class="anno" data-anno="passAt1">pass@1</button> measures
            whether one sampled answer is correct. <button class="anno" data-anno="cons">cons@16 or cons@64</button>
            measures whether repeated samples can be combined by consensus. R1-Zero's strong pass@1 means the model is
            not relying only on majority voting; consensus then adds another layer of test-time compute.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">From raw reasoning to usable assistant</span>
            <p>
              R1-Zero is strong on math and code but weak on instruction following and preference benchmarks. Cold-start
              and SFT improve usability but can temporarily reduce raw reasoning. Later RL restores reasoning, and final
              mixed RL mainly improves user-facing benchmarks such as AlpacaEval and ArenaHard.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="failures" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Failed routes</h2>

          <p>
            the final design easier to understand: alternatives such as <button class="anno" data-anno="prm">PRM</button> and <button class="anno" data-anno="mcts">MCTS</button> may look more elegant,
            but GRPO plus outcome reward is more scalable.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Attempt</th>
                  <th>Why it was attractive</th>
                  <th>What went wrong</th>
                  <th>Lesson</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {failureRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.method}</td>
                    <td>{row.promise}</td>
                    <td>{row.problem}</td>
                    <td>{row.lesson}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is the paper's deeper engineering philosophy: do not choose the method that gives the most beautiful
            supervision signal on paper; choose the method whose feedback is reliable and whose training loop can scale.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="limitations" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Limits and product-report parts</h2>

          <p>
            DeepSeek-R1 is also a public model report, not only a technical paper about R1-Zero. That is why the
            paper spends space on safety, deployment, distillation, cost, and release details. These parts are useful
            for understanding the model as a released system, but they are not the central mechanism behind how
            GRPO elicits reasoning.
          </p>

          <div class="comparison-grid">
            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Safety</strong>
              <p>
                Stronger reasoning is a double-edged sword. The same ability that helps a model break down a math
                problem can also make unsafe requests more dangerous, because the model may produce more concrete,
                executable plans.
              </p>
              <p>
                This is why the report includes a long safety appendix and discusses an external risk-control system.
                For the technical story of R1-Zero, safety is not the main mechanism, but for a public model release it
                is part of the system-level picture.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Token efficiency</strong>
              <p>
                Long chain-of-thought is one of the main behaviors encouraged by RL. On hard problems, more thinking
                tokens can give the model room to verify, backtrack, and try alternative strategies.
              </p>
              <p>
                The limitation is overthinking. If the model spends thousands of tokens on simple questions, reasoning
                becomes slow, expensive, and sometimes less user-friendly. A better reasoning model should learn when
                to think long and when to answer directly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Tools</strong>
              <p>
                R1 reasons mainly inside text. It does not natively use search engines, calculators, compilers, or other
                external tools as part of its reasoning loop.
              </p>
              <p>
                This matters because many real tasks should not be solved by internal reasoning alone. Search can check
                fresh facts, calculators can prevent arithmetic mistakes, and compilers can verify code. Tool-augmented
                RL is therefore a natural next step beyond R1-style text-only reasoning.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Distillation</strong>
              <p>
                <button class="anno" data-anno="distillation">Distillation</button> is not the core proof of the paper.
                The core proof is R1-Zero: a strong base model can develop reasoning behavior through RL on verifiable
                tasks.
              </p>
              <p>
                But distillation is a major release contribution. Once a large model has learned R1-style reasoning,
                its generated trajectories can train smaller Qwen and Llama models. In that sense, distillation turns the
                expensive RL result into something more accessible for the open-model ecosystem.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

