            <p>
              PPO estimates advantage with a separate value model, then combines reward, value estimates, and a KL constraint to
              update the policy. GRPO removes the value model. For each question, it samples a group of outputs, scores them,
              and computes <em>relative</em> advantages within the group. This is why the “G” in GRPO stands for
              <em>group</em>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">What PPO is doing</span>
            <p>
              PPO is a standard policy optimization method. In the LLM setting, the policy model generates an output, a reward
              model scores that output, and a value model estimates how good the trajectory is expected to be. Advantage is then
              computed with techniques such as GAE, and the policy is updated while staying close to a frozen reference model by
              a KL penalty.
            </p>
            <p>
              The strength of PPO is that it is general. The weakness, for this paper, is that the value model becomes costly
              and brittle when reasoning traces are long. In long chain-of-thought settings, it is hard to estimate the value of
              a partially generated solution: a response may look wrong halfway through and later self-correct, or look
              promising early and still fail at the end.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">What GRPO changes</span>
            <p>
              GRPO keeps the policy optimization flavor of PPO, but replaces the value-model-based advantage estimator with a
              simpler group-relative one. For the same question <em>q</em>, the old policy samples a group of outputs
              <em>o</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>o</em><sub>2</sub>, …, <em>o</em><sub>G</sub>. Each output gets a reward
              <em>r</em><sub>i</sub>. GRPO then compares each reward to the group mean and normalizes by the group standard
              deviation:
            </p>

            <div class="formula-card">
              <code>
                A<sub>i</sub> =
                (r<sub>i</sub> − mean(r<sub>1</sub>, ..., r<sub>G</sub>))
                / std(r<sub>1</sub>, ..., r<sub>G</sub>)
              </code>
            </div>

            <p>
              So advantage no longer means “how good is this output in absolute terms?” It means “how good is this output
              relative to the other attempts for the same question?” Outputs above the group average get positive advantage and
              become more likely; outputs below average get negative advantage and become less likely.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">Why this matters for DeepSeek-R1</span>
            <p>
              This design fits R1-Zero well. The paper mainly trains on verifiable tasks such as math, code, STEM, and logic.
              Those tasks give relatively clear outcome rewards: the answer is correct or not, the code passes tests or not,
              the format is valid or not. GRPO can therefore skip a large value model and still get a usable training signal by
              comparing a batch of attempts to the same problem.
            </p>
            <p>
              In other words, DeepSeek-R1 does not use GRPO because GRPO is fashionable. It uses GRPO because, for long
              reasoning with reliable outcome rewards, group-relative comparison is cheaper, simpler, and better matched to the
              problem than value estimation in PPO.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">The intuition in one sentence</span>
            <p>
              PPO asks a value model to predict how promising a trajectory is; GRPO lets several attempts at the same question
              compete against one another and uses that relative ranking as the learning signal.
            </p>
          </div>
